/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - An ignition system has four jobs: make a voltage high enough to jump the plug gap; send it to the cylinder that is ready; fire at the right crank angle, a little before top dead centre (TDC), because the flame takes time to build pressure; 
   - The battery-and-coil system does all four.
   - When the points close, current builds in the primary winding, a few hundred turns of thick wire, and sets up a magnetic field in the iron core.
   - e is the induced emf in volts (V), N the number of turns, Φ the magnetic flux in webers (Wb) and t the time in seconds (s).
   - The same core links both windings, so the collapse induces an emf in both.
   - Dwell is the period for which the points stay closed and current flows in the primary.
   - Ts is the spark interval in seconds, N the engine speed in rev/min and z the number of cylinders.
   - The coil current does not jump to its full value.
   - i is the primary current in amperes after the points have been closed for t seconds, V the supply voltage, R the total primary resistance in ohms, L the primary inductance in henries and τ the time constant in seconds.
   - With points the dwell angle is fixed by the cam, so at high speed the dwell time shrinks and the spark weakens (worked example 2).
*/
